# Valence orbital ionisation energies, for the energy axis of a
# qualitative MO correlation diagram.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR.  A correlation diagram puts the central atom's
# orbitals on one side and the terminal atoms' symmetry orbitals on the
# other, and the whole point of the picture is that they sit at
# DIFFERENT heights: the more electronegative fragment lower, so the
# bonding combination comes out closer to it and the reader can see
# which end of the bond the electrons are on.  Drawing both sides at an
# arbitrary height would make the diagram symmetric and teach the wrong
# thing.
#
# These are valence orbital potential energies, in eV, negative -- the
# "orbital potential energy" table a textbook correlation diagram is
# drawn from (Miessler & Tarr and the inorganic texts that follow it).
#
# NOT the extended-Huckel H_ii parameters, which are a different set
# fitted for a different purpose and are NOT monotonic across a period:
# mixing the two is how this file's first draft ended up with sulphur's
# 3p above phosphorus's.  One source, or the trends stop meaning
# anything.  They are also NOT the calculation's own
# orbital energies: the centre column of the diagram carries those, and
# the two axes are deliberately not the same thing.  A fragment orbital
# has no computed energy unless the fragment calculation is actually
# run, which is the option this was chosen over.
#
# PROVENANCE AND ACCURACY.  Sources differ by a few tenths of an eV.
# What a teaching diagram needs from them is the ORDERING and the rough
# gaps, not the third significant figure.  Check them against your own
# course table before teaching from the numbers themselves.
#
# Elements are listed only where a standard published value was
# available.  An element that is absent simply gets no energy and no
# fragment level, which is the honest outcome; filling the gaps by
# interpolating the trend would produce numbers that look like data.
#
# The trends are enforced mechanically in tests/symmetry, which is what
# catches a transcription slip: within a period both levels become more
# negative left to right, and ns always lies below np on the same atom.
# A sign error or a transposed pair of digits breaks one of those.
#
# FORMAT.  One line per element:
#
#     <symbol>  <n>  <s energy>  <p energy>  <valence electrons>
#
# where <n> is the valence principal quantum number and the energies are
# eV.  An element with no p value in its valence shell writes "-".
#
# The valence electron count is the group number, and it is what puts
# arrows on the fragment levels: a diagram without them does not say
# which combinations are filled, which is half of what it is for.  The
# molecular charge is added to (or taken from) the total before filling,
# so NO2- gets its extra electron.

H    1    -13.6        -   1

# Second period.
Li   2     -5.4     -3.5   1
Be   2     -9.3     -6.0   2
B    2    -14.0     -8.3   3
C    2    -19.4    -10.7   4
N    2    -25.6    -13.2   5
O    2    -32.4    -15.9   6
F    2    -40.2    -18.7   7

# Third period.
Na   3     -5.1     -3.0   1
Mg   3     -7.6     -4.5   2
Al   3    -11.3     -6.0   3
Si   3    -15.0     -7.8   4
P    3    -18.7    -10.1   5
S    3    -22.7    -11.6   6
Cl   3    -25.3    -13.7   7

